\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Heat kernels as compact gauge transfer weights}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Let #{G} be a compact connected Lie group, equipped with a
positive Ad-invariant inner product on its real Lie algebra.
Let #{\covsq_G\le0} be the Laplace operator for the resulting
bi-invariant metric. For #{\tau>0}, let #{k_\tau} be the heat
kernel started at the identity, as a density against Haar
probability #{\mu_G}, with convention
##{\partial_\tau k_\tau=\tfrac12\covsq_G k_\tau.}
For each complex unitary irreducible #{\rho}, let #{c_\rho\ge0}
be the eigenvalue of #{-\covsq_G} on its matrix coefficients, and set
##{a_\rho(\tau)=\ue^{-\tau c_\rho /2}.}
The character coefficient of the heat kernel is the finite identity
##{\int_G k_\tau(h)\overline{\chi_\rho(h)}\,d\mu_G(h)
 =\dim\rho a_\rho(\tau).}
In particular #{a_{\mathrm{triv}}(\tau)=1}. For every pair of finite
sets #{F\subset F'} of these irreducibles, the truncations
##{s_{\tau,F}(g)=\sum_{\rho\in F}\dim\rho a_\rho(\tau)\chi_\rho(g)}
satisfy the exact bound
##{|s_{\tau,F'}(g)-s_{\tau,F}(g)|
 \le\sum_{\rho\in F'\setminus F}(\dim\rho)^2a_\rho(\tau),}
with equality at #{g=e}. For every such finite #{F}, this
truncation is the kernel #{k_F} of
\vocabk{Spatial link fields and their transfer kernel}{fdip-007Z}
with these coefficients.}

\proof{
\p{The Ad-invariant metric makes conjugations isometries fixing
#{e}, so uniqueness of the heat kernel makes #{k_\tau} central.
Inversion is also such an isometry for a bi-invariant metric,
which gives #{k_\tau(g^{-1})=k_\tau(g)}. The density is real.
The same uniqueness makes #{k_\tau} a probability density:
the constant function #{1} solves the heat equation, so
#{\int_G k_\tau\,d\mu_G=1}.}

\p{By definition of #{c_\rho}, the character satisfies
#{\covsq_G\chi_\rho=-c_\rho\chi_\rho}. For smooth #{f}, set
##{(K_\tau f)(g)=\int_G k_\tau(gh^{-1})f(h)\,d\mu_G(h).}
For #{\tau} bounded away from #{0} the density is smooth, so
differentiation under the integral uses the heat equation in the
first variable and gives
#{\partial_\tau K_\tau f=\tfrac12\covsq_G K_\tau f}.
As #{\tau} decreases to #{0}, #{K_\tau f} returns #{f} for
continuous #{f}. The function #{\ue^{-\tau c_\rho /2}\chi_\rho}
has the same limit and solves the heat equation. Their difference
#{u_\tau} is smooth for #{\tau>0}, tends to #{0}, and solves the
heat equation. On a compact manifold the spatial maximum of such
a solution cannot increase: at a maximum point
#{\covsq_G u_\tau\le0}, so #{\partial_\tau u_\tau\le0}. The same
bound for #{-u_\tau} forces #{u_\tau=0}. Therefore
#{K_\tau\chi_\rho=\ue^{-\tau c_\rho /2}\chi_\rho}.}

\p{Evaluate at the identity. The substitution #{h\mapsto h^{-1}}
and the identity #{\chi_\rho(h^{-1})=\overline{\chi_\rho(h)}} give
the displayed character coefficient. The trivial character is
#{1} and #{c_{\mathrm{triv}}=0}, so the integral of #{k_\tau}
gives #{a_{\mathrm{triv}}(\tau)=1}. The real Laplacian commutes
with complex conjugation, so #{c_{\rho^*}=c_\rho} and
#{a_{\rho^*}(\tau)=a_\rho(\tau)}. A finite set closed under dual
representations therefore has a real inversion-symmetric
truncation.}

\p{Unitarity gives #{|\chi_\rho(g)|\le \dim\rho=\chi_\rho(e)}.
Every coefficient #{\dim\rho a_\rho(\tau)} is positive. The
difference #{s_{\tau,F'}-s_{\tau,F}} is the sum over
#{F'\setminus F}, so the triangle inequality is the displayed
bound, and at #{e} every term is positive and the bound is an
equality.}
}

\p{Remark. Continuum comparison. The convergent series and the
#{L^2} setting are not the statement above. The Brownian motion
generated by #{\covsq_G/2} has zero drift and no jumps. Its
positive-time density is smooth and hence in #{L^2(G,\mu_G)} by
compactness. Lévy's Proposition 4.2.3 gives strict positivity
#{k_\tau(g)>0} for every #{g}
\citet{pp. 137–138}{levy2010holonomy}. Equations (53)–(55) give
##{k_\tau(g)=\sum_{\rho\in\widehat G}\dim\rho a_\rho(\tau)\chi_\rho(g),}
uniform absolute convergence, and
##{\sum_{\rho\in\widehat G}(\dim\rho)^2a_\rho(\tau)=k_\tau(e)<\infty}
\citet{pp. 136–137}{levy2010holonomy}. Connectedness is used for
that pointwise positivity: on a disconnected Lie group, Brownian
motion started at #{e} stays in the identity component. To match
the reference measure, let #{v_G} be the total Riemannian volume
and let #{h_\tau} be the density against that volume. Since
#{d\mu_G=d\mathrm{vol}_G/v_G}, one has #{k_\tau=v_Gh_\tau}. Lévy's
unit-volume convention
\citet{§3.1, p. 107}{levy2010holonomy} is implemented by changing
the density, without rescaling the metric, #{\covsq_G} or
#{c_\rho}. The finite truncations above are the partial sums of
this comparison series. Pointwise positivity is not claimed for
a finite truncation.}
